      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Certificate-level info:**\n- Number: R49/2006-CZ-11.01\n- Member state: Czech Republic\n- Issuing authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, CZ\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Date issued: 28 June 2011 → 2011-06-28\n- CIML Member: Pavel Klenovsky\n- Pages: 7 pages\n- Recommendation: R 49, edition 2006, accuracy class 1 and 2\n- Test report: No. 6015-PT-P0076-11, 41 pages (plus annexes)\n- Scheme: This is an OIML certificate of conformity; typical scheme is A or B. The document doesn't explicitly state scheme A or B. Rule: don't fabricate. I'll omit scheme or set null. Certificate has \"scheme (A|B)\" — the doc doesn't mention it, so omit.\n\n**Applicant / Manufacturer:**\n- Emerson Process Management / Rosemount Flow Division\n- 12001 Technology Drive, Eden Prairie, 553 44 MN, USA\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Measuring system\" — actually this is a water meter / magnetic flow meter. The category options include \"Gas meter\", \"Measuring system\"... For R49 (water meters), category might be \"Water meter\" or \"Measuring system\". The description says \"Magnetic Flow Meter\". R49 covers \"Water meters\". Hmm. The certificate says \"Identification of the certified type: Magnetic Flow Meter\". I'll use \"Water meter\" or \"Magnetic flow meter\". The schema examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\". For R49 water meter, I'd say \"Water meter\". The certificate describes: \"The water meters are intended for metering cold and hot potable water.\" So \"Water meter\" seems fitting. But the certified type identification is \"Magnetic Flow Meter\". I'll use \"Magnetic flow meter\" as the category... Hmm, the schema says category like examples. Since R49 is water meters and the description calls them water meters, but the type identification says \"Magnetic Flow Meter\". Let me use \"Water meter\" — actually, let me be safe and use the exact identification: \"Magnetic Flow Meter\". The category field says e.g. with \"...\" allowing other categories. I'll use \"Magnetic flow meter\" since that's the literal identification of the certified type.\n\n- type_designations: [\"8732\", \"8705\", \"8711\"] — Transmitter type 8732, Flow Sensor types 8705 and 8711.\n\n**Components:**\n- Transmitter (measuring transducer / electronic calculator) type 8732\n- Flow sensor type 8705 (standard flanges)\n- Flow sensor type 8711 (flangeless/wafer)\n\nRoles: transmitter is \"Measuring transducer with electronic calculator\" (8732). Sensors: 8705 and 8711 — alternatives? The cert covers both sensor types. The system consists of flow sensor types 8705 and 8711 and transmitter 8732. Are the two sensors alternatives (\"OR\")? A given meter uses either 8705 or 8711. The tables: Page 3 table (8711 with PTFE lining) — DN 4-40. Page 4 table (8711, DN 50-200). Page 5-7 tables are 8705 (polyurethane and PTFE lining), DN 15-900. Actually let's check: Page 3 header: \"Magnetic Flow Meter Transmitter types 8732 and Magnetic Flow Sensor types 8711 with the lining material made by PTFE\" — connection type: Wafer. Page 4 continues DN 50-200, connection type Wafer. Page 5: \"Transmitter types 8732 and Magnetic Flow Sensor types 8705 with the lining material made by Polyurethane and PTFE\" — connection: Standard flanges. Page 6-7 continue 8705.\n\nSo:\n- 8711 (wafer): DN 4, 8, 15, 25, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200\n- 8705 (flanges): DN 15, 25, 40, 50, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900\n\nComponents entries:\n1. role: \"transmitter\" / \"Measuring transducer with electronic calculator\", type_designations: [\"8732\"]\n2. role: \"flow_sensor\", type_designations: [\"8705\", \"8711\"], alternatives: \"OR\"? Well, each meter has one sensor; both types are covered. Since a given configuration uses one or the other, alternatives: \"OR\" is reasonable.\n\n**Model family:**\nThe cert covers many DN variants across two sensor types. Model variants can be keyed by sensor type + DN. E.g., \"8711 DN4\", \"8711 DN8\", ..., \"8705 DN15\", ... This is a big matrix.\n\nShould I build model_level characteristics for each DN? That's a lot but doable. The tables are essentially per-DN columns. Attributes per DN: q4 (overload flowrate), q3 (permanent flowrate), q2 (transitional flowrate), q1 (minimum flowrate), ratio q3/q1, accuracy class, length, resolution, max admissible pressure, etc.\n\nLet me structure:\n\nmatrix_tables: 5 tables (page 3, 4, 5, 6, 7). Each with columns and rows per DN. Actually each table has rows = attributes, columns = DN. I'll convert: rows per DN.\n\nTable 1 (page 3): 8711 PTFE, DN 4, 8, 15, 25, 40.\n- q4: ≤0.50, ≤2.0, ≤7.9, ≤20.0, ≤50.0\n- q3: ≤0.40, ≤1.6, ≤6.3, ≤16.0, ≤40.0\n- q2: ≥0.00512, ≥0.0205, ≥0.0504, ≥0.128, ≥0.320\n- q1: ≥0.00320, ≥0.0128, ≥0.0320, ≥0.080, ≥0.200\n- ratio q3/q1: ≤125 (DN4,8), ≤200 (DN15,25,40) — footnote 2\n- ratio q2/q1: 1.6\n- ratio q4/q3: 1.25\n- accuracy class: 2\n- MPE_l: ±5%\n- MPE_u: ±2% (≤30°C), ±3% (>30°C)\n- temperature classes: T50 polyurethane, T90 PTFE\n- max admissible pressure: 0 to 19 bar (DN4,8,15), 0 to 50 (DN25,40) — colspan 3 covers DN4,8,15; colspan 2 covers DN25,40\n- max pressure loss: 0.16 bar\n- environmental classes: B and C\n- electromagnetic environment: E1 and E2\n- indicating range [m³]: 9 999 to 99 999\n- resolution of indicating device [L]: 0.01 (DN4,8), 0.1 (DN15,25), 1 (DN40)\n- min straight length inlet: 5\n- min straight length outlet: 3\n- orientation: arbitrary\n- length [mm]: 55, 55, 56, 57, 73\n- connection: Wafer\n- Power: AC Umax 250, Umin 90, freq 50 or 60; DC Umax 42, Umin 12\n\nTable 2 (page 4): 8711 PTFE, DN 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200.\n- q4: ≤78.8, ≤125, ≤200, ≤313, ≤788, ≤1250\n- q3: ≤63.0, ≤100, ≤160, ≤250, ≤630, ≤1000\n- q2: ≥0.504, ≥1.60, ≥2.56, ≥4.0, ≥10.1, ≥16.0\n- q1: ≥0.315, ≥1.00, ≥1.60, ≥2.5, ≥6.3, ≥10.0\n- ratio q3/q1: ≤100 (footnote 2)\n- ratio q2/q1: 1.6\n- ratio q4/q3: 1.25\n- accuracy class: 2 (DN50), 1 (DN65-200)\n- MPE_l: ±5% (DN50), ±3% (others)\n- MPE_u: DN50: ±2%/±3%; others: ±1%/±2%\n- temperature classes: T50/T90\n- environmental: B and C\n- electromagnetic: E1 and E2\n- max admissible pressure: 0 to 50 bar\n- max pressure loss: 0.16 bar\n- indicating range [m³]: 999 999 to 9 999 999\n- resolution [L]: 1 (DN50,65,80,100), 10 (DN150,200)\n- min straight inlet: 5, outlet: 3\n- length [mm]: 84, 120, 149, 180, 230 (DN150,200 both 230)\n- connection: Wafer\n- Power same.\n\nTable 3 (page 5): 8705, PU and PTFE, DN 15, 25, 40, 50, 80.\n- q4: ≤7.9, ≤20.0, ≤50.0, ≤78.8, ≤200\n- q3: ≤6.3, ≤16.0, ≤40.0, ≤63.0, ≤160\n- q2: ≥0.0504, ≥0.128, ≥0.320, ≥0.504, ≥2.56\n- q1: ≥0.0320, ≥0.080, ≥0.200, ≥0.315, ≥1.60\n- ratio q3/q1: ≤200 (DN15,25,40,50), ≤100 (DN80)\n- ratio q2/q1: 1.6\n- ratio q4/q3: 1.25\n- accuracy class: 2 (DN15-50), 1 (DN80)\n- MPE_l: ±5% (DN15-50), ±3% (DN80)\n- MPE_u: ±2%/±3% (DN15-50), ±1%/±2% (DN80)\n- temp classes: T50/T90\n- environmental: B and C\n- EM: E1, E2\n- max admissible pressure: 0 to 50 bar\n- max pressure-loss: 0.16 bar\n- indicating range [m³]: 99 999 to 999 999\n- resolution [L]: 0.1 (DN15,25), 1 (DN40,50,80)\n- min straight inlet: 5, outlet: 2\n- orientation: arbitrary\n- length [mm]: 200 all\n- connection: Standard flanges\n- Power same.\n\nTable 4 (page 6): 8705, DN 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350.\n- q4: ≤313, ≤788, ≤1250, ≤2000, ≤3125, ≤3125\n- q3: ≤250, ≤630, ≤1000, ≤1600, ≤2500, < 2500\n- q2: ≥4.0, ≥10.1, ≥16.0, ≥25.6, ≥40.0, ≥50.0\n- q1: ≥2.5, ≥6.3, ≥10.0, ≥16.0, ≥25.0, ≥31.3\n- ratio q3/q1: ≤100 (DN100-300), <80 (DN350)\n- ratio q2/q1: 1.6\n- ratio q4/q3: 1.25\n- accuracy class: 1\n- MPE_l: ±3%\n- MPE_u: ±1%/±2%\n- temp classes: T50/T90\n- env: B, C\n- EM: E1, E2\n- max admissible pressure: 0 to 50\n- max pressure loss: 0.16\n- indicating range [m³]: 999 999 to 9 999 999\n- resolution [L]: 1 (DN100), 10 (DN150-300), 100 (DN350)\n- min straight inlet 5, outlet 2\n- length [mm]: 250, 300, 350, 435, 512, 588\n- connection: Standard flanges\n\nTable 5 (page 7): 8705, DN 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900.\n- q4: ≤3125, ≤3125, ≤5000, ≤5000, ≤7875, ≤12500\n- q3: ≤2500, ≤2500, <4000, ≤4000, ≤6300, ≤10000\n- q2: ≥63.5, ≥80.0, ≥101.6, ≥128.0, ≥201.6, ≥320.0\n- q1: ≥39.7, ≥50.0, ≥63.5, ≥80.0, ≥126.0, ≥200.0\n- ratio q3/q1: ≤63 (DN400), ≤50 (DN450), ≤63 (DN500), ≤50 (DN600,750,900)\n- ratio q2/q1: 1.6\n- ratio q4/q3: 1.25\n- accuracy class: 1\n- MPE_l: ±3%\n- MPE_u: ±1%/±2%\n- temp: T50/T90\n- env: B,C\n- EM: E1,E2\n- max admissible pressure: 0 to 40\n- max pressure loss: 0.16\n- indicating range [m³]: 9 999 999 (probably max)\n- resolution [L]: 100\n- min straight inlet 5, outlet 2\n- length [mm]: 664, 761, 839, 1000, 1200, 1200\n- connection: Standard flanges\n\nFootnotes:\n1) The value of Q3 shall be chosen from the R5 line of ISO 3:1973.\n2) The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen from the R10 line from ISO 3:1973 and this value shall be higher than 10.\n\n**model_family:**\nfamily_name: null. Models: each sensor-type + DN combo. 8711: DN 4, 8, 15, 25, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200 (11 models). 8705: DN 15, 25, 40, 50, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900 (17 models). Total 28 models.\n\nmodel_id format: \"8711-DN4\", \"8711-DN8\", ..., \"8705-DN15\", etc.\n\n**model_level characteristics:** For each attribute, values per model. This will be long but correct. Attributes:\n- overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h): max values (≤ means max). Should I encode as {min:null, max:0.5}? The \"≤\" indicates upper bound. I'll encode value as {min: null, max: X}. Hmm, that might be noisy but per rules open ranges use null for missing bound: ≥1000 → {min:1000, max:null}. So ≤0.50 → {min:null, max:0.5}. OK.\n- permanent_flowrate_q3: similar with footnote marker \"1)\".\n- transitional_flowrate_q2: {min:X, max:null}.\n- minimum_flowrate_q1: {min:X, max:null}.\n- ratio_q3_q1: {max:...} with footnote 2. DN-specific.\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6 — same across all models → could be type_level.\n- ratio_q4_q3: 1.25 — type_level.\n- accuracy_class: 2 for 8711 DN4-50; 1 for 8711 DN65-200; 2 for 8705 DN15-50; 1 for 8705 DN80-900. Model level.\n- length_mm: model level.\n- resolution_l: model level.\n- max_admissible_pressure_bar: 0-19 for 8711 DN4,8,15; 0-50 for 8711 DN25,40 and all 8705 except page 7 (0-40). Page 7: 8705 DN400-900: 0-40. Model level.\n- indicating_range_m3: varies by table group. Model level or matrix tables only. Keep in matrix tables; maybe include in model_level too. I'll include as model_level with value {min,max}: 9999-99999 for 8711 DN4-40; 999999-9999999 for 8711 DN50-200; 99999-999999 for 8705 DN15-80; 999999-9999999 for 8705 DN100-350; 9999999 (single, likely max) for 8705 DN400-900. Hmm the page 7 \"9 999 999\" alone — probably indicating range max. I'll record as 9999999 (value only? Ambiguous). I'll put value 9999999 with note? Rules say don't fabricate. I'll put {min:null, max:9999999}? That's interpretation. Actually \"Indicating range [m³]: 9 999 999\" — the range is just up to 9999999? Possibly the max reading. I'll encode as {min: null, max: 9999999}. Hmm, risky. Alternatively keep as string \"9999999\". I think encoding as max-only is reasonable but could be wrong. Let me keep it in matrix_tables rows as 9999999 and skip model_level for that group... but model_level should be consistent. I'll encode value as 9999999 (number) in matrix row and in model_level put {min: null, max: 9999999}? I'll just use number 9999999 in matrix tables and for model_level use the same number. Actually a \"range\" characteristic with a single value — I'll use {max: 9999999, min: null}. Hmm. Let me simplify: in model_level I'll include indicating_range only where min-max given. For page 7 models, value 9999999 as a plain number. That's honest to the doc.\n\nActually, to reduce risk and size, maybe I should keep indicating_range, resolution, temperature classes etc. in matrix_tables only, and populate model_level for the key flow attributes (q4, q3, q2, q1, ratio q3/q1, accuracy class, length, resolution, max pressure). The schema wants matrix tables preserved AND model_level populated. Let me do:\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n1. overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h)\n2. permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h) with footnote \"1)\"\n3. transitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h)\n4. minimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h)\n5. ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless, footnote 2)\n6. accuracy_class (dimensionless)\n7. meter_length (mm)\n8. indicating_device_resolution (L)\n9. maximum_admissible_pressure (bar) — {min, max}\n10. mpe_upper_zone_t30 / temperature-conditional? That varies by accuracy class group... MPE_u: ±2%/±3% for class 2; ±1%/±2% for class 1. Could add config_level with axis \"water_temperature\" and condition. Actually MPE depends on temperature: two conditions (≤30°C, >30°C). This is a config axis, not model. config_level: attribute \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone\", axis \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition:\"≤30°C\" wait condition should reference temperature}...]. But the MPE values also differ per accuracy class (5%/2%/3% for class 2 vs 3%/1%/2% for class 1). So MPE depends on class which depends on model. Hmm.\n\nMaybe simpler: config_level entries:\n- attribute: mpe_lower_zone, axis: accuracy_class, values: [{condition: \"class 2\", value: {min:-5,max:5}, unit:\"%\"}, {condition: \"class 1\", value: {min:-3,max:3}, unit:\"%\"}]\n- attribute: mpe_upper_zone, axis: water_temperature within accuracy class... two axes. Could do conditions like \"class 2, water ≤30°C\": ±2%; \"class 2, water >30°C\": ±3%; \"class 1, ≤30°C\": ±1%; \"class 1, >30°C\": ±2%.\n\nThat works with condition strings.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6\n- ratio_q4_q3: 1.25\n- maximum_pressure_loss: 0.16 bar\n- environmental_classes: \"B and C\" → value \"B, C\" string. Structured: value as string \"B and C\". OK.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1 and E2\"\n- temperature_classes: \"T50 for polyurethane liner; T90 for PTFE liner\" — string.\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 5 (dimensionless, likely ×DN)\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe: varies! Page 3-4: 3; page 5: 2; page 6: 2; page 7: 2. 8711: 3; 8705: 2. Model level? It varies by sensor type. I could make it model_level grouped by sensor type or config_level. Simpler: model_level attribute minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe with values per model (3 for 8711 models, 2 for 8705 models). That's 28 entries. Alternatively config_level with axis sensor_type. I'll use model_level for it — actually that adds bulk. Could do config_level: axis \"sensor_type\": {condition: \"8711\", value: 3}, {condition: \"8705\", value: 2}. That's compact and accurate. Similarly inlet: 5 both — type_level.\n\n- orientation_limitation: \"Arbitrary orientation\" — type_level string.\n- connection_type: Wafer for 8711; Standard flanges for 8705 — config_level axis sensor_type.\n- power supply: type AC: Umax 250 V, Umin 90 V, frequency 50 or 60 Hz; type DC: Umax 42 V, Umin 12 V — type_level. Frequency 50 or 60 → {min:50, max:60} Hz.\n- accuracy_classes (recommendation-level): 1 and 2.\n\nAlso the note that Q3 values chosen from R5 line of ISO 3:1973 — footnote 1. Q3/Q1 from R10, >10 — footnote 2.\n\n**Certificate fields:**\n- number: \"R49/2006-CZ-11.01\"\n- scheme: not stated → omit\n- project_number: not stated (test report 6015-PT-P0076-11 — could be project? No, that's test report). Omit.\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: \"2011-06-28\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not stated. Omit.\n\n**issuing_authority:**\n- name: Czech Metrology Institute\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"CZ\"]\n- person_responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- person_title: \"The Issuing Authority\" — hmm. He signs as issuing authority. Title could be omitted. There's also CIML Member Pavel Klenovsky. The schema has person_responsible, person_title. I'll leave person_title out or null. Actually maybe omit.\n\n**applicants:** one entry.\n\n**manufacturers:** same as applicant.\n\n**test_reports:**\n- id: \"6015-PT-P0076-11\", pages: 41, role: \"type_evaluation_report\" (it's \"Test report\"). Role: test_report. \"includes 41 pages (plus annexes)\" → pages: 41. Date not given.\n\n**recommendation:**\n- id: \"R49\", edition: 2006, accuracy_classes: [1, 2]. Wait accuracy classes here are 1 and 2 (water meter classes). scheme? The schema has scheme under recommendation too — omit.\n\n**Components:**\n- {role: \"transmitter\", type_designations: [\"8732\"], characteristics: {...}} — description: \"Measuring transducer with electronic calculator\" — remote mount or compact design.\n- {role: \"flow_sensor\", type_designations: [\"8705\"], characteristics: {connection_type: wafer? no—8705 is flanged}}. Wait: standard flanges (type 8705), flangeless wafer (type 8711).\n- So components: \n  1. role \"measuring_transducer_calculator\", type_designations [\"8732\"]\n  2. role \"flow_sensor\", type_designations [\"8705\", \"8711\"], alternatives \"OR\"\n\nThe certificate text: \"The water meters consist of flow sensor types 8705 and 8711 and of measuring transducer with the electronic calculator type 8732.\" Each meter uses one sensor type, so alternatives OR.\n\n**Lining materials:** polyurethane or PTFE. 8711 tables: PTFE only. 8705 tables: Polyurethane and PTFE.\n\n**Description:** from page 2 measuring system description. I'll include condensed description in certified_type.description.\n\nNow matrix_tables. I'll produce 5 tables, one per page (3,4,5,6,7). Name them:\n- \"Characteristics of 8732 + 8711 (PTFE liner), DN 4–40\"\n- \"Characteristics of 8732 + 8711 (PTFE liner), DN 50–200\"\n- \"Characteristics of 8732 + 8705 (PU and PTFE liner), DN 15–80\"\n- \"Characteristics of 8732 + 8705 (PU and PTFE liner), DN 100–350\"\n- \"Characteristics of 8732 + 8705 (PU and PTFE liner), DN 400–900\"\n\nColumns: model, dn, q4, q3, q2, q1, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class, indicating_range, resolution, max_admissible_pressure, meter_length, mpe_l, mpe_u_le30, mpe_u_gt30. Hmm, rows as objects per DN. Keep columns: [\"model\", \"dn_mm\", \"q4_m3_h\", \"q3_m3_h\", \"q2_m3_h\", \"q1_m3_h\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"accuracy_class\", \"meter_length_mm\", \"resolution_l\", \"max_admissible_pressure_bar\", \"indicating_range_m3\"]. Column_units maybe. The schema example includes column_units. I'll include units array.\n\nValues in rows: numbers where possible. q4 ≤0.50 → record 0.5 with note it's an upper bound? For matrix rows, maybe I keep the numbers plain (the ≤/≥ is understood as bounds). But rule 2 says structured values for characteristics; matrix rows in example are plain numbers (qmax: 4.0). The R137 example uses plain numbers in rows. So plain numbers in matrix rows is fine. But ≤ vs ≥ matters; the row labels can indicate \"overload_flowrate_q4_max\" etc. I'll name columns \"q4_max\", \"q3_max\", \"q2_min\", \"q1_min\". Good.\n\nratio_q3_q1: values like 125, 200, 100, 80, 63, 50 with footnote 2. Store as numbers in rows (with footnote in table footnotes).\n\nFootnotes per table: footnote 1 and 2 texts.\n\n**model_level values:** For each attribute, 28 values. Let me list all models and their data.\n\nModels (sensor, DN):\n8711: 4, 8, 15, 25, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200\n8705: 15, 25, 40, 50, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 750, 900\n\nq4 (max, m³/h):\n- 8711 DN4: 0.5\n- 8711 DN8: 2.0\n- 8711 DN15: 7.9\n- 8711 DN25: 20.0\n- 8711 DN40: 50.0\n- 8711 DN50: 78.8\n- 8711 DN65: 125\n- 8711 DN80: 200\n- 8711 DN100: 313\n- 8711 DN150: 788\n- 8711 DN200: 1250\n- 8705 DN15: 7.9\n- 8705 DN25: 20.0\n- 8705 DN40: 50.0\n- 8705 DN50: 78.8\n- 8705 DN80: 200\n- 8705 DN100: 313\n- 8705 DN150: 788\n- 8705 DN200: 1250\n- 8705 DN250: 2000\n- 8705 DN300: 3125\n- 8705 DN350: 3125\n- 8705 DN400: 3125\n- 8705 DN450: 3125\n- 8705 DN500: 5000\n- 8705 DN600: 5000\n- 8705 DN750: 7875\n- 8705 DN900: 12500\n\nq3 (max, m³/h), footnote 1:\n- 8711: 0.4, 1.6, 6.3, 16.0, 40.0, 63.0, 100, 160, 250, 630, 1000\n- 8705: 6.3, 16.0, 40.0, 63.0, 160, 250, 630, 1000, 1600, 2500, 2500(<), 2500, 2500, 4000(<), 4000, 6300, 10000\n\nNote: DN350 8705 q3 is \"< 2500\" and DN500 is \"< 4000\". Those are strict less-than. Encoding: value {max: 2500}? The doc says \"< 2500\". I'll record as number 2500 in matrix but in model_level use {min:null, max:2500}. The distinction < vs ≤ is minor; I'll keep {max:2500}. Fine.\n\nq2 (min, m³/h):\n- 8711: 0.00512, 0.0205, 0.0504, 0.128, 0.320, 0.504, 1.60, 2.56, 4.0, 10.1, 16.0\n- 8705: 0.0504, 0.128, 0.320, 0.504, 2.56, 4.0, 10.1, 16.0, 25.6, 40.0, 50.0, 63.5, 80.0, 101.6, 128.0, 201.6, 320.0\n\nq1 (min, m³/h):\n- 8711: 0.00320, 0.0128, 0.0320, 0.080, 0.200, 0.315, 1.00, 1.60, 2.5, 6.3, 10.0\n- 8705: 0.0320, 0.080, 0.200, 0.315, 1.60, 2.5, 6.3, 10.0, 16.0, 25.0, 31.3, 39.7, 50.0, 63.5, 80.0, 126.0, 200.0\n\nratio_q3_q1 (max, footnote 2):\n- 8711 DN4: 125, DN8: 125, DN15: 200, DN25: 200, DN40: 200, DN50-200: 100\n- 8705 DN15: 200, DN25: 200, DN40: 200, DN50: 200, DN80: 100, DN100-300: 100, DN350: 80 (<80), DN400: 63, DN450: 50, DN500: 63, DN600: 50, DN750: 50, DN900: 50\n\naccuracy_class:\n- 8711 DN4-50: 2\n- 8711 DN65-200: 1\n- 8705 DN15-50: 2\n- 8705 DN80-900: 1\n\nmeter_length (mm):\n- 8711 DN4: 55, DN8: 55, DN15: 56, DN25: 57, DN40: 73\n- 8711 DN50: 84, DN65: 120, DN80: 149, DN100: 180, DN150: 230, DN200: 230\n- 8705 DN15-80: 200 (all five)\n- 8705 DN100: 250, DN150: 300, DN200: 350, DN250: 435, DN300: 512, DN350: 588\n- 8705 DN400: 664, DN450: 761, DN500: 839, DN600: 1000, DN750: 1200, DN900: 1200\n\nresolution (L):\n- 8711 DN4: 0.01, DN8: 0.01, DN15: 0.1, DN25: 0.1, DN40: 1\n- 8711 DN50: 1, DN65: 1, DN80: 1, DN100: 1, DN150: 10, DN200: 10\n- 8705 DN15: 0.1, DN25: 0.1, DN40: 1, DN50: 1, DN80: 1\n- 8705 DN100: 1, DN150: 10, DN200: 10, DN250: 10, DN300: 10, DN350: 100\n- 8705 DN400-900: 100\n\nWait check page 5 resolution: colspan 2 → DN15, 25 → 0.1; colspan 3 → DN40, 50, 80 → 1. Yes.\nPage 6: DN100 → 1; colspan 4 → DN150, 200, 250, 300 → 10; DN350 → 100. Yes.\n\nmax_admissible_pressure (bar, range min-max):\n- 8711 DN4: {0, 19}, DN8: {0,19}, DN15: {0,19}, DN25: {0,50}, DN40: {0,50}\n- 8711 DN50-200: {0,50}\n- 8705 DN15-350: {0,50}\n- 8705 DN400-900: {0,40}\n\nindicating_range (m³):\n- 8711 DN4-40: {9999, 99999}\n- 8711 DN50-200: {999999, 9999999}\n- 8705 DN15-80: {99999, 999999}\n- 8705 DN100-350: {999999, 9999999}\n- 8705 DN400-900: 9999999 (single value)\n\nMPE values — config_level:\n- mpe_lower_zone: axis accuracy_class: class 2 → ±5%, class 1 → ±3%\n- mpe_upper_zone: axis water_temperature within accuracy_class:\n  - class 2, ≤30 °C → ±2%\n  - class 2, >30 °C → ±3%\n  - class 1, ≤30 °C → ±1%\n  - class 1, >30 °C → ±2%\n\nFor MPE ±X% → {min: -X, max: X} unit \"%\". Good.\n\nOther type-level:\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6\n- ratio_q4_q3: 1.25\n- maximum_pressure_loss: 0.16 bar\n- temperature_classes: value string \"T50 (polyurethane liner); T90 (PTFE liner)\" — better keep close to doc: \"T50 for polyurethane liner; T90 for PTFE liner\". Unit null.\n- environmental_classes: \"B and C\" → \"B, C\"? Keep \"B and C\".\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1 and E2\"\n- orientation_limitation: \"Arbitrary orientation\"\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 5\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe: config_level axis sensor_type: 8711 → 3, 8705 → 2.\n- connection_type: config_level axis sensor_type: 8711 → \"Wafer\", 8705 → \"Standard flanges\".\n- power supply: \n  - power_supply_type_ac_voltage_max: hmm. Better as separate characteristics:\n    - ac_supply_voltage_max: 250 V\n    - ac_supply_voltage_min: 90 V\n    - ac_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz\n    - dc_supply_voltage_max: 42 V\n    - dc_supply_voltage_min: 12 V\n  Or use value ranges: power_supply_voltage_ac {90–250 V}. Doc gives Umax and Umin separately. I'll do:\n    - ac_power_supply_voltage: {min:90, max:250}, unit V\n    - ac_power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60}, unit Hz\n    - dc_power_supply_voltage: {min:12, max:42}, unit V\n  That