butes in example. So partial population is acceptable.\n\nI'll populate model_level with: overload_flowrate_q4, permanent_flowrate_q3, transitional_flowrate_q2, minimum_flowrate_q1, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class, meter_length, indicating_device_resolution, maximum_admissible_pressure. Nine attributes.\n\nValues format in model_level: each value must be structured object {value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers}. Per schema example: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}. The schema example for model_level shows just value without unit object. Rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol shape. The R137 example shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — plain. Conflict. I'll follow rule 2 strictly: values: {model, value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers}? The layered schema:\n\n```\nmodel_level[]:\n    attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}\n```\n\nAnd attribute has `unit`. So per-attribute unit once. And values {model, value, footnote_markers}. I'll use {model, value, footnote_markers} with attribute-level unit_symbol. For bounds values, value is object {min, max}.\n\nLet me now define the structured entries:\n\nmodel_level entries:\n\n1. attribute: \"overload_flowrate_q4\", unit_symbol: \"m³/h\", values: per model {value: {min:null, max:X}}. Since the attribute label includes \"overload\" and it's an upper bound, maybe just value: X with understanding? Rule: ≤0.50 → {min:null, max:0.5}. I'll apply the range encoding.\n\nHmm, this doubles verbosity but rules demand it. Actually for ≥ values (q1, q2) → {min: X, max: null}. OK.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the example in rule 4 for R60 shows \"maximum_capacity\" plain values 25, 30... because those are exact. For flowrates with ≤/≥, encode bounds. Fine.\n\n2. permanent_flowrate_q3: unit m³/h, footnote 1 on all values.\n3. transitional_flowrate_q2.\n4. minimum_flowrate_q1.\n5. ratio_q3_q1: unit null (dimensionless), footnote 2 on all.\n6. accuracy_class: unit null,